# PostgreSQL clusters (CloudNativePG)

The CloudNativePG (CNPG) operator and its Barman Cloud plugin run in
`cnpg-system` (`kubernetes/infrastructure/controllers/cnpg/`). Each tenant runs
its own one-instance cluster in its own namespace; the cluster side provides
the operator, the RBAC, the `tenant-local` StorageClass and the alert rules.

## Decisions

- One CNPG cluster per app, so each app's point-in-time recovery is its own.
- Backups through the Barman Cloud plugin `ObjectStore`, not the deprecated
  in-tree `barmanObjectStore`.
- Instance volumes come from StorageClass `tenant-local` (`tenant_storage`):
  one `local` volume per claim, a directory on the `tenant-volumes` zvol of
  the NAS-colocated agent, kept when its claim is deleted. A `local` volume
  applies the pod's `fsGroup` (26), which `hostPath` ignores. The provisioner
  does not enforce sizes, so each tenant namespace carries a storage quota.
- `enablePDB: false` on one instance: an unsatisfiable PodDisruptionBudget
  makes every node drain time out.

## What the operator adds per tenant

No volume: the tenant's claim provisions its own from `tenant-local`. The
tenant's wiring file carries a storage quota, because the provisioner does
not enforce sizes on the shared zvol:

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: <app>-quota
  namespace: <namespace>
spec:
  hard:
    persistentvolumeclaims: "4"
    tenant-local.storageclass.storage.k8s.io/requests.storage: 40Gi
```

A tenant that also dumps its database needs a backup landing zone on the NAS,
registered in three places the way authentik's is:

- an NFS PersistentVolume pre-bound by `claimRef` to the dump claim, with
  `storageClassName: ""` and `persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain`
  ([kubernetes/apps/authentik/storage.yaml](../kubernetes/apps/authentik/storage.yaml));
- a per-app `nas_storage_exports` entry with its own `fsid` and
  `xprtsec: "tls"`
  ([ansible/inventories/prod/group_vars/nas.yml](../ansible/inventories/prod/group_vars/nas.yml),
  the `/export/backups-apps/authentik` entry);
- a `nas_storage_backup_artifact_apps` entry in the same file, plus the
  matching `absent(backup_artifact_last_mtime_seconds{app="<app>"})` arm on
  `BackupArtifactStale`
  ([kube-prometheus-stack/release.yaml](../kubernetes/infrastructure/observability/kube-prometheus-stack/release.yaml)).

## What the tenant ships (example)

Every object below lives in the tenant's own repository and namespace, with
no `namespace:` set (the tenant Kustomization's `targetNamespace` supplies it).
The connection-Secret contract is weisssrv-lib's docs/DATABASE-CONTRACT.md,
in releases v0.22.0 and later.

```yaml
apiVersion: barmancloud.cnpg.io/v1
kind: ObjectStore
metadata:
  name: <app>-backups
  annotations:
    kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/prune: disabled
spec:
  retentionPolicy: "30d"
  configuration:
    destinationPath: s3://<bucket>/
    endpointURL: https://<s3 endpoint>
    s3Credentials:
      accessKeyId: {name: <app>-backups, key: access-key-id}
      secretAccessKey: {name: <app>-backups, key: secret-access-key}
    wal: {compression: gzip, maxParallel: 2}
    data: {compression: gzip}
  instanceSidecarConfiguration:
    env:
      - {name: AWS_REQUEST_CHECKSUM_CALCULATION, value: when_required}
      - {name: AWS_RESPONSE_CHECKSUM_VALIDATION, value: when_required}
---
apiVersion: postgresql.cnpg.io/v1
kind: Cluster
metadata:
  name: <app>-db
  annotations:
    kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/prune: disabled
spec:
  instances: 1
  enablePDB: false
  plugins:
    - name: barman-cloud.cloudnative-pg.io
      isWALArchiver: true
      parameters: {barmanObjectName: <app>-backups}
  storage:
    storageClass: tenant-local
    size: 20Gi
---
apiVersion: postgresql.cnpg.io/v1
kind: ScheduledBackup
metadata:
  name: <app>-db-nightly
spec:
  schedule: "0 0 11 * * *"
  immediate: true
  backupOwnerReference: self
  cluster: {name: <app>-db}
  method: plugin
  pluginConfiguration: {name: barman-cloud.cloudnative-pg.io}
```

The object-store Secret `<app>-backups` comes from an ExternalSecret against
the tenant's store, keys `access-key-id` and `secret-access-key`. Instance pods
must admit `cnpg-system` on 8000, `observability` on 9187 and their own
application pods on 5432, and reach DNS, the apiserver at
`${cluster_apiserver_egress_cidr}` on 6443 and public 443; a PodMonitor on
`cnpg.io/cluster: <app>-db`, port `metrics`, feeds the rules.
Ship an `absent(cnpg_collector_up{namespace="<namespace>"})` rule too; the
platform rules need a series to fire.

## Point-in-time recovery

Recover into a side cluster in a scratch namespace, check it, then cut over.

1. Pick the target time `T` (RFC 3339, UTC). The side cluster's volume
   comes from `tenant-local` like any other claim.
2. Create the scratch namespace and copy the object-store Secret and
   `ObjectStore` into it, the `ObjectStore` without `retentionPolicy`.
3. Create the side cluster with no `plugins:` section, so it never archives
   into the production path:

```yaml
apiVersion: postgresql.cnpg.io/v1
kind: Cluster
metadata:
  name: <side>
spec:
  instances: 1
  enablePDB: false
  bootstrap:
    recovery:
      source: production
      recoveryTarget: {targetTime: "<T>"}
  externalClusters:
    - name: production
      plugin:
        name: barman-cloud.cloudnative-pg.io
        parameters: {barmanObjectName: <app>-backups, serverName: <app>-db}
  storage:
    storageClass: tenant-local
    size: 20Gi
```

4. `kubectl -n <scratch namespace> wait --for=condition=Ready cluster/<side> --timeout=60m`,
   then compare row counts of the tables that matter, and a marker row written
   just before `T`, against production.
5. Cut over by restoring the application's database from the side cluster
   (`pg_dump` from the side, restore into production after stopping writers),
   or by re-pointing the application at a cluster bootstrapped the same way.
6. Delete the scratch namespace, then the side volume it leaves `Released`:
   read its directory with `kubectl get pv <pv> -o jsonpath='{.spec.local.path}'`,
   delete the PersistentVolume, and remove that directory on the storage node.

Run the drill before an application holds data that matters, and quarterly.

## Runbooks

### Postgres down

Alerts: `CNPGPostgresDown`. Run `kubectl cnpg status -n <ns> <cluster>` and
`kubectl -n <ns> logs <cluster>-1 -c postgres --tail=200`. A full volume shows
as `No space left on device`; a node reboot explains a short outage.

### Database metrics absent

Alerts: tenant `DatabaseAbsent`, the tenant's own
`absent(cnpg_collector_up{namespace="<namespace>"})` rule. No series means
none of the platform rules above can fire.

1. Check that the instance pod exists and is scheduled:
   `kubectl -n <ns> get pods,cluster.postgresql.cnpg.io` and
   `kubectl -n <ns> describe pod <cluster>-1`.
2. Check the PodMonitor target on port `metrics` (9187) in Prometheus.
3. Check that the instance pods' ingress NetworkPolicy (`db-allow-ingress` in
   the app-template) admits `observability` on 9187.
4. Once the pod runs, continue with [Postgres down](#postgres-down).

### WAL archiving failing

Alerts: `CNPGWALArchivingFailing`. Read
`kubectl -n <ns> logs <cluster>-1 -c plugin-barman-cloud --tail=200`: an expired
object-store key, an object store refusing checksum headers (set the two
`AWS_*_CHECKSUM_*` variables above to `when_required`), or no public egress.

### Base backup failed or stale

Alerts: `CNPGBaseBackupFailed`, `CNPGBaseBackupStale`, `CNPGBaseBackupStaleCritical`.
`kubectl -n <ns> get backups.postgresql.cnpg.io`, describe the newest failed
one, fix the cause, then
`kubectl cnpg backup -n <ns> <cluster> --method=plugin --plugin-name=barman-cloud.cloudnative-pg.io`.
